I kept the first version very direct. For every starting position, the code extends the substring until a character appears for the second time. A small boolean array is reset for each new start.

For example, with `abcabcbb`, starting at index 0 gives `abc` before the next `a` stops that scan. The same check is then done from the next starting position.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
  public:
    int longestNonRepeatingSubstring(string& s){
        int answer = 0;

        for (int start = 0; start < static_cast<int>(s.size()); start++) {
            bool seen[256] = {};

            for (int end = start; end < static_cast<int>(s.size()); end++) {
                unsigned char current = static_cast<unsigned char>(s[end]);

                if (seen[current]) {
                    break;
                }

                seen[current] = true;
                answer = max(answer, end - start + 1);
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Dry run for `pwwkew`: the scan from index 0 finds `pw`, then stops at the second `w`. A later scan finds `wke`, so the result becomes 3.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case. Space complexity: `O(1)` for the fixed character table.

A `HashSet` makes it easy to know which characters are inside the current window. The right side keeps moving forward. When it meets a character already in the set, the left side removes characters until the duplicate is gone.

The input string is not changed. This version is easy to follow, although the left pointer may move several times for one right pointer position.

```java
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

class Solution {
    public int longestNonRepeatingSubstring(String s) {
        Set<Character> window = new HashSet<>();
        int left = 0;
        int longest = 0;

        for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
            char current = s.charAt(right);

            while (window.contains(current)) {
                window.remove(s.charAt(left));
                left++;
            }

            window.add(current);
            longest = Math.max(longest, right - left + 1);
        }

        return longest;
    }
}
```

The empty string returns 0 naturally. A string such as `aaaa` keeps a window of length 1, while a string with all different characters keeps expanding to its full length.

Time complexity: `O(n)` on average because each character enters and leaves the set at most once. Space complexity: `O(min(n, k))`, where `k` is the number of possible different characters.

This one uses a frequency array instead of a set. The current character is counted when the right pointer reaches it. If its count becomes 2, the left pointer moves forward and reduces counts until the window is valid again.

The array has room for standard 8-bit characters, so no hash table is needed. It is a handy choice when the character range is known.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
  public:
    int longestNonRepeatingSubstring(string& s){
        int count[256] = {};
        int left = 0;
        int longest = 0;

        for (int right = 0; right < static_cast<int>(s.size()); right++) {
            unsigned char current = static_cast<unsigned char>(s[right]);
            count[current]++;

            while (count[current] > 1) {
                unsigned char leaving = static_cast<unsigned char>(s[left]);
                count[leaving]--;
                left++;
            }

            longest = max(longest, right - left + 1);
        }

        return longest;
    }
};
```

For `abcabcbb`, the first three characters produce a window of length 3. When the next `a` arrives, the code removes characters from the left until only one `a` remains in the window.

An empty string needs no special branch and returns 0. The code reads the string only, so it does not modify the input.

Time complexity: `O(n)`. Space complexity: `O(1)` because the count array always has 256 entries.

